INT. CAFE ON A BRIDGE, PARIS - RAINY AFTERNOON

A narrow glass cafe built into the span of a stone bridge. Steamed windows. The river slides past below, brown and slow. Six small tables, a zinc counter, a cracked espresso machine.

CELINE ARCHAMBEAU-DIALLO (30s, red scarf, damp hair) sits at the corner table with a book she is not reading. Her coffee has gone cold.

At the counter, the owner, MONSIEUR FERNAND LAGRANGE-OBI (60s, white apron, forearms like cables), wipes the same glass for the fifth time.

The bell above the door chimes. In steps ISAAC BERGMAN-NWANKWO (30s, battered trench coat, a violin case in one hand, rain running off the brim of his hat).

Celine looks up. Looks down. Turns a page.

Isaac stops. Takes off his hat. Holds it in both hands.

FERNAND
Monsieur?

ISAAC
Just a coffee.

FERNAND
Sit anywhere.

Isaac looks at the corner table. The one empty chair across from Celine. He sits at the next table over instead. Sets the violin case on his lap.

The silence has weight. Fernand brings the coffee. He sets it down, pauses, glances at Celine, then at Isaac, and retreats behind the counter with the expression of a man who has seen this before.

Celine turns another page without reading it. Isaac stirs his coffee for a very long time. Neither of them has added sugar.

ISAAC (CONT'D)
(not looking)
You cut your hair.

CELINE
(not looking)
You grew a beard.

ISAAC
It's raining.

CELINE
I noticed.

His hand rests on the violin case. Her thumb traces the edge of a page. Fernand polishes the glass again.

CELINE (CONT'D)
Is it still in tune?

ISAAC
Why?

CELINE
Just asking.

He opens the case. Lifts the violin. Tucks it under his chin. Draws the bow across a single string, long and low, and the sound fills the narrow cafe, and Celine closes her book.
